The [[1985–86 in Scottish football|1985–86 Scottish football season]] was to be [[David Hay|Davie Hay's]] 3rd season in charge after winning his first trophy, the [[Scottish Cup]] the previous season, Celtic were looking to compete for the [[Scottish Premier Division]] league title<ref>{{Cite news |last=Davidson |first=Alan |date=23 July 1985 |title=Hay's top priority |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=A9JAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=1KYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=3532%2C4002452 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=32}}</ref> that [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]] had won the two previous seasons under Manager [[Alex Ferguson]].

== Season Overview ==
The club would begin the season with a new chairman, Thomas Devlin<ref>{{Cite news |last=Davidson |first=Alan |date=21 August 1985 |title=Following the leaders |url=https://books.google.es/books?id=bdhAAAAAIBAJ&lpg=PA20&dq=thomas%20devlin%20celtic&pg=PA20#v=onepage&q&f=false |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=51}}</ref> who had been a director since 1949 took over the job from [[Desmond White (footballer)|Desmond White]] who died suddenly while on holiday in Greece during the summer.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Petrie |first=Gordon |date=22 June 1985 |title=Desmond White dies on Crete holiday |url=https://books.google.es/books?id=qe89AAAAIBAJ&lpg=PA1&dq=desmond%20white%20crete&pg=PA1#v=onepage&q=desmond%20white%20crete&f=false |work=[[The Herald (Glasgow)]] |pages=1}}</ref> Desmond White an important figure in the clubs history, had been chairman since 1971, when he took over from [[Robert Kelly (football chairman)|Sir Robert Kelly]], he had been a director since 1947 when he succeeded his father Thomas White on the board of directors.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Davidson |first=Alan |date=22 June 1985 |title=Celtic dynasty may go on |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=TdJAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=2KYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=3680%2C4698217 |url-status=live |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=24}}</ref>

Celtic were linked with several players during the summer, especially strikers to replace cup winning hero [[Frank McGarvey]] who was surprisingly sold to [[St Mirren F.C.|St Mirren]] just after scoring the winning goal in the [[1985 Scottish Cup final|1985 Scottish Cup Final]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Young |first=Chick |date=22 May 1985 |title=Why I must leave Celtic |url=https://books.google.es/books?id=G9JAAAAAIBAJ&lpg=PA21&dq=frank%20McGarvey%20transfer&pg=PA21#v=onepage&q=frank%20McGarvey%20transfer&f=false |work=[[The Herald (Glasgow)]] |pages=40}}</ref> Full backs [[Graeme Sinclair]] and [[Mark Reid]] had already departed in May. The club was linked with various players during the pre-season, speculation included players such as [[Pat Nevin]]<ref>{{Cite news |last=Young |first=Chick |date=2 July 1985 |title=Celtic and me |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=8dFAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=1KYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=5424%2C309130 |url-status=live |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=28}}</ref> and [[Frank Gray]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Young |first=Chick |date=13 July 1985 |title=Celts could bid |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=-9FAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=1KYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=2373%2C2426539 |url-status=live |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=24}}</ref> It would take until November for Celtic to make a signing when they brought in ex Aberdeen hero [[Mark McGhee]] from German Bundesliga team [[Hamburger SV|Hamburg]].

=== August ===
The domestic season began with a draw away to Hearts at [[Tynecastle Park|Tynecastle]], followed by consecutive league victories. Celtic's [[1985–86 Scottish League Cup|League Cup]] campaign opened with two comfortable wins against lower-league opposition, defeating [[Queen of the South F.C.|Queen of the South]] and [[Brechin City F.C.|Brechin City]]. August concluded with a strong performance at Celtic Park against Rangers, with the Old Firm encounter ending in a draw after a closely contested match.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=dthAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=0qYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=4209%2C6077230|title=Macs are goal boys|last=Davidson|first=Alan|date=31 August 1985|pages=1|url-status=live|work=[[The Glasgow Times]]}}</ref>

=== September ===
September began with disappointment in the [[1985–86 Scottish League Cup|League Cup]]. Celtic were eliminated following a dramatic 4–4 draw after extra time away to Hibernian, before losing the subsequent penalty shoot-out. The team responded positively in league competition, recording three wins from three matches to maintain momentum in the early stages of the season. Celtic's European campaign in the European Cup Winners’ Cup also began during the month, with a challenging first-round draw against Atlético Madrid. Celtic achieved a creditable 1–1 draw in the first leg away in Madrid. September 1985 was also marked by tragedy with the death of former Celtic and Scotland manager Jock Stein. Stein collapsed and died while attending Scotland's World Cup qualifying match, in which the national team secured the point required to reach the play-offs for the [[1986 FIFA World Cup|1986 FIFA World Cup in Mexico]].

=== October ===
Celtic's [[1985–86 European Cup Winners' Cup|European campaign]] came to an end in October following a poor second-leg performance against Atlético Madrid, played behind closed doors.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Davidson |first=Alan |date=3 October 1985 |title=Celtic's horror repeat |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=sNdAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=tKYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=6093%2C719558 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=39}}</ref> The sanction had been imposed by UEFA as a result of crowd disturbances during the previous season's fixture against Rapid Wien in the UEFA Cup.<ref>{{Cite web |last=McDevitt |first=James |date=8 December 2025 |title=Celtic’s Mid-Eighties Watershed – Celtic v Atletico Madrid, 1985 |url=https://thecelticstar.com/celtics-mid-eighties-watershed-celtic-v-atletico-madrid-1985/ |website=The Celtic Star}}</ref> Domestically, Celtic began the month at the top of the league table<ref>{{Cite news |last=Young |first=Chick |date=4 October 1985 |title=Paradise again |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=sddAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=tKYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=3792%2C1069182 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=29}}</ref> but experienced inconsistent form. Victories over St Mirren and Clydebank were offset by home defeats to Hearts and Dundee United<ref>{{Cite news |last=Young |first=Chick |date=26 October 1985 |title=Celts hit the skids |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=w9dAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=tKYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=3544%2C6359561 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=1}}</ref> These results caused Celtic to relinquish first place, and they finished the month second in the table behind Aberdeen on goal difference.

=== November ===
Inconsistent league form continued into November, with the team opening the month with consecutive defeats. A heavy loss away to league leaders Aberdeen at Pittodrie.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Young |first=Chick |date=2 November 1985 |title=Frank the destroyer |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=zOJAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=xaYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=2880%2C499824 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=1}}</ref> was followed by a home defeat to a dominant Rangers side.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Davidson |first=Alan |date=9 November 1985 |title=Ian is the Ibrox ace |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=0uJAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=xaYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=3064%2C2026999 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=1}}</ref> These results saw Celtic fall to third place in the table, three points behind Aberdeen and one point adrift of Rangers.<ref>{{Cite news |date=9 November 1985 |title=Results |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=0uJAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=xaYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=3740%2C2172843 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=28}}</ref> A home victory over Clydebank, with both goals scored by new signing [[Mark McGhee]], helped stabilize results<ref>{{Cite news |last=Paul |first=Ian |date=18 November 1985 |title=McGhee's double brings instant relief |url=https://news.google.co.uk/newspapers?id=VsBAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=1aUMAAAAIBAJ&pg=3856%2C3786592 |work=[[The Herald (Glasgow)]] |pages=12}}</ref> However, a subsequent draw away to Hibernian delayed any sustained return to form.

=== December ===
December began with a hard-fought draw against an improving Hearts side, a result that saw them drop to fourth place in the league. Aberdeen remained top of the table, with Hearts in second and Rangers narrowly ahead of Celtic on goal difference. A scheduled fixture against [[Motherwell F.C.|Motherwell]] was postponed due to heavy rain.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Traynor |first=John |date=21 December 1985 |title=Rain K.O. for the Celts |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=Uh0-AAAAIBAJ&sjid=RUoMAAAAIBAJ&pg=5180%2C4811516 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=1}}</ref> Celtic returned to league action on Monday, 23 December, suffering a 1–0 defeat away to Dundee United. The result further weakened their position in the title race, leaving the club in fifth place and four points behind leaders Hearts, although Celtic had played three matches fewer.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Davidson |first=Alan |date=24 December 1985 |title=Get it right |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=Ux0-AAAAIBAJ&sjid=RUoMAAAAIBAJ&pg=5770%2C5426968 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=48}}</ref> Celtic ended the calendar year with a victory over [[Clydebank F.C. (1965)|Clydebank]], moving back into fourth position and setting the stage for a crucial New Year's Day encounter with [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] at [[Celtic Park]].

=== January ===
Celtic began the New Year with a 2–0 victory over rivals [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]], a result that revitalized their league campaign and left Rangers trailing in the championship race.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Young |first=Chick |date=3 January 1985 |title=Celts on upswing |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=8hI-AAAAIBAJ&sjid=FEoMAAAAIBAJ&pg=5708%2C246558 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=42}}</ref> Despite the win, Celtic still faced a challenge to close the gap on [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]], [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Hearts]], and [[Dundee United F.C.|Dundee United]], who occupied the positions above them in the table. In their next fixture, Celtic were defeated 4–2 by a strong [[Dundee United F.C.|Dundee United]] side;<ref>{{Cite news |last=Young |first=Chick |date=3 January 1985 |title=Red-hot United Shatter Celts |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=8xI-AAAAIBAJ&sjid=FEoMAAAAIBAJ&pg=6328%2C587408 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=28}}</ref> however, this proved to be their final league loss of the season. Two subsequent draws against [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]]<ref>{{Cite news |last=Davidson |first=Alan |date=11 January 1985 |title=Peter the great |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=lNpAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=w6YMAAAAIBAJ&pg=3431%2C1797064 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=1}}</ref> and [[Hibernian F.C.|Hibernian]] slowed momentum, but a home victory over [[Motherwell F.C.|Motherwell]] helped preserve their title ambitions. The home victory against [[Motherwell F.C.|Motherwell]] would prove to be [[David Provan (footballer, born 1956)|Davie Provan's]] last game before having to retire due to illness.,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Paul |first=Ian |date=2 July 1987 |title=Mixed feelings as Walker joins up and McClair signs off |url=https://books.google.es/books?id=PKw1AAAAIBAJ&lpg=PA15&dq=davie%20provan&pg=PA15#v=onepage&q=davie%20provan&f=false |work=[[The Herald (Glasgow)]] |pages=16}}</ref> Celtic's [[1985–86 Scottish Cup|Scottish Cup campaign]] also commenced in January, beginning with a 2–0 win over [[St Johnstone F.C.|St Johnstone]].

=== February ===
February began with a comeback win away to [[Dundee F.C.|Dundee]] which kept Celtic in the title race, 4 points off leaders [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Hearts]] but with 2 games in hand. On Monday 3 February Celtic moved to sack assistant manager [[Frank Connor]], this news was quite a shock especially to the players.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Young |first=Chick |date=3 February 1986 |title=Connor's Agony |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=MtpAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=wqYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=6428%2C552167 |url-status=live |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=32}}</ref> A slim win The next two league matches saw a dip in league form which would see Celtic draw against [[St Mirren F.C.|St. Mirren]] and [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Hearts]] both games at home.

=== March ===
[[1985–86 Scottish Cup|Scottish Cup]] participation ended at the beginning of March with a last gasp defeat 4-3 defeat against [[Hibernian F.C.|Hibernian]] at Easter Road,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Davidson |first=Alan |date=8 March 1986 |title=Celts cup of woe |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=CNhAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=y6YMAAAAIBAJ&pg=4850%2C1828210 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=1}}</ref> this was followed by two draws, dropping points to [[Dundee United F.C.|Dundee United]] away in a 1-1 draw, then a dramatic 4-4 draw at [[Ibrox Stadium|Ibrox]] against old rivals [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] saw [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] stutter again in the title race, now in 4th place 7 points behind leaders [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Hearts]], the league title was looking increasingly out of reach if recent form continued.<ref>{{Cite news |date=22 March 1986 |title=Results |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=uAc-AAAAIBAJ&sjid=okkMAAAAIBAJ&pg=3541%2C5632917 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=28}}</ref> Finally after a run of four league games without a win, Celtic won the final game of the month away to Clydebank with a convincing second half performance to deliver a 0-5 away win and 2 crucial points.

=== April ===
Now out of all the Cup competitions, Celtic had only the league title left to challenge for. April would see Celtic face a marathon six league fixtures, all crucial in the race for the League Championship. Celtic began the month sitting in 4th place on 36 points, 9 points behind leaders Hearts, who had 45 points, Dundee Utd sat 2nd on 40 points and 3rd placed Aberdeen on 38 points. Celtic did have 3 games in hand over the league leaders but it would be a challenge to close the gap.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2 April 1986 |title=Results |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=GthAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=y6YMAAAAIBAJ&pg=2548%2C7451951 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=39}}</ref> A win at home midweek to Dundee was followed by a last gasp away win to St. Mirren, thanks to improvised striker [[Paul McStay]]'s 87th minute goal, helped Celtic continue a winning run and close the gap with league leaders Hearts to 5 points.<ref>{{Cite news |date=5 April 1986 |title=Results |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=ct5AAAAAIBAJ&sjid=2aYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=3157%2C1241847 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=27}}</ref> The following Saturday Celtic claimed a win with a stylish victory over Champions Aberdeen at Pittodrie, a Mo Johnston goal was enough to claim full points,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Davidson |first=Alan |date=12 April 1986 |title=Touch of Mo Magic |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=eN5AAAAAIBAJ&sjid=2aYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=4088%2C2692831 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=1}}</ref> but Hearts won convincingly 3-0 away to fellow title contenders Dundee United, keeping the gap at 5 points and still looking like favorites to clinch the title with only four games left.<ref>{{Cite news |date=12 April 1986 |title=Results |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=eN5AAAAAIBAJ&sjid=2aYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=3015%2C2852666 |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=28}}</ref> A win against Hibs at home, thanks to two late goals, kept Celtic with some faint hope of catching leaders Hearts, this was followed by another win at home against Dundee. Celtic were now 4 points behind with two league fixtures left, Hearts had played one more game and would not play until the last day of the season away to Dundee at the start of May. Celtic won their game in hand a midweek victory away to Motherwell, setting up a last day of the season tie away to St. Mirren, hoping that Hearts would lose and that Celtic would win by 4 goals. This would be enough to clinch Davie Hay's first League title as Manager and Celtic's first since the 1982/83 season.

=== May ===
Saturday 3 May 1986 is considered one of the most dramatic finishes to a Championship race, Celtic went 4-0 up by half time, but Hearts will still 0-0 at Dens Park against Dundee, this would mean the title was still going to Tynecastle for the first time in 26 years. Another goal by Brian McClair in the 54th minute gained a cheer from the massive travelling Celtic support but it was nothing compared to the noise and joy that would break out amongst the Celtic fans when they got news that Dundee had scored in the 83rd minute thanks to Albert Kidd, and once Kidd made it 2-0 to Dundee in the 86th minute Celtic fans began to celebrate a dramatic title win.

== Pre-season and friendlies ==
Celtic prepared for the 1985–86 season with a summer tour Sweden during late July and early August, playing 4 games during a weeks trip.{{football box collapsible|id=GIF Sundsvall v Celtic|date=27 July 1985|time=|round=[[Exhibition game#Club football|Friendly]]|score=1-3|report=https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1986-07-27-to-07-31-pre-season-ireland/|team1=[[GIF Sundsvall]] {{flagicon|SWE}}|goals1=|team2={{flagicon|SCO}} Celtic|goals2=[[Brian McClair|McClair]] {{goal|28}}, [[Alan McInally|McInally]] {{goal|63}} {{goal|75}}|stadium=[[Idrottsparken (Sundsvall)]]|location=[[Sundsvall]], Sweden|attendance=3,500|referee=|result=W}}{{football box collapsible|id=Älgarna-Härnösand IF v Celtic|date=31 July 1985|time=|round=[[Exhibition game#Club football|Friendly]]|score=0-11|report=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=XdhAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=0qYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=3675%2C188646|team1=[[Älgarna-Härnösand IF]] {{flagicon|SWE}}|goals1=|team2={{flagicon|SCO}} Celtic|goals2=|stadium=Ängevallen|location=[[Härnösand]], Sweden|attendance=|referee=|result=W}}{{football box collapsible|id=Sollefteå GIF v Celtic|date=3 August 1985|time=|round=[[Exhibition game#Club football|Friendly]]|score=1–1|report=[https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1987-07-23-tidaholms-gif-1-2-celtic-friendly/ Report]|team1=Sollefteå GIF {{flagicon|SWE}}|goals1=|team2={{flagicon|SCO}} Celtic|goals2=|stadium=Nipvallen|location=[[Sollefteå]], Sweden|attendance=|referee=|result=D}}{{football box collapsible|id=Örnsköldsvik v Celtic|date=5 August 1985|time=|round=[[Exhibition game#Club football|Friendly]]|score=0-1|report=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=YdhAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=0qYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=3846%2C1081303|team1=Örnsköldsvik {{flagicon|SWE}}|goals1=[[Brian McClair|McClair]] {{goal|24}}|team2={{flagicon|SCO}} Celtic|goals2=[[Murdo MacLeod|McLeod]] {{goal|85}}|stadium=|location=[[Örnsköldsvik]], Sweden|attendance=|referee=|result=W}}

